From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000002 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Philippe Simons <simons.philippe@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 4 Jul 2026 14:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] fix: sanitize GAMESCOPE_COLOR_NIGHT_MODE atom

The arm64 Steam client packs this property wrong for Xlib's format=32 ABI, and the
hue never reaches the compositor.

XChangeProperty(format=32) takes a long* on LP64 and transmits the low 32 bits of
each element. The client hands it a float[3] {amount, hue, saturation} -- 12 bytes
-- so Xlib reads three 8-byte longs off a 12-byte buffer: long[0] is {amount, hue}
and its low half is amount, long[1] is {saturation, ...} and its low half is
saturation, and long[2] is entirely past the end of the buffer. The wire vector is
therefore [amount, saturation, overread].

That is confirmed on hardware by sweeping each UI control and watching the atom:
the tint slider drives vec[0], "Peak Saturation" drives vec[1] (0.5..1.0), and
"Primary Hue" moves nothing. The hue is computed client-side and then destroyed in
transit, which is why that slider is inert and why no compositor-side change can
revive it. vec[2] is an out-of-bounds read of the client's stack, not an
uninitialised field -- hence garbage that is re-rolled per install (1.02e15 and
6.5e22 seen, -6.25527e-27 on 2026-08-04).

So the stock decode is wrong twice. Reading vec[1] as the hue turns night mode into
a magenta/red screen instead of warm amber. Reading vec[2] as the saturation is
worse than wrong, it is a coin flip: a large positive clamps to 1.0 and works by
luck, while a negative clamps to 0.0, which multiplies the tint away so night mode
does nothing at all at any intensity. That is why "night mode does nothing" was
never reproducible across cards.

This was first read as a consequence of this panel exposing no EDID. The packing
analysis rules that route out for good: the vector is mangled before it ever leaves
the client, so no amount of advertised colorimetry would change what arrives.

Take the two fields that survive the packing, pin the hue the wire loses to amber,
and guard both against non-finite values -- the only validation on this path is the
size()==3 check, so garbage would otherwise reach the HSV math unchecked. Night mode
becomes a reliable warm cast whose strength follows the tint slider and whose
intensity follows the saturation slider. "Primary Hue" stays inert.
---
 src/steamcompmgr.cpp | 36 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 36 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/steamcompmgr.cpp b/src/steamcompmgr.cpp
--- a/src/steamcompmgr.cpp
+++ b/src/steamcompmgr.cpp
@@ -6349,6 +6349,42 @@
 		nightmode.hue = vec[1];
 		nightmode.saturation = vec[2];

+		// novadeck: the arm64 Steam client packs this property wrong for Xlib's format=32
+		// ABI. XChangeProperty(format=32) takes a long* on LP64 and transmits the low 32 bits
+		// of each element. The client hands it a float[3] {amount, hue, saturation} -- 12
+		// bytes -- so Xlib reads three 8-byte longs off a 12-byte buffer:
+		//     long[0] = {amount, hue}      -> low half = amount
+		//     long[1] = {saturation, ...}  -> low half = saturation
+		//     long[2] = past the buffer    -> whatever the client's stack held
+		// The wire vector is therefore [amount, saturation, overread]. The hue IS computed
+		// client-side and then destroyed in transit, and vec[2] is an out-of-bounds read --
+		// which is why it is re-rolled per install (1.02e15 and 6.5e22 seen, -6.25527e-27 on
+		// 2026-08-04) rather than merely uninitialised. HW-confirmed by sweeping each control:
+		// the tint slider drives vec[0], "Peak Saturation" drives vec[1] (0.5..1.0), and
+		// "Primary Hue" moves nothing -- that slider cannot be fixed from here.
+		//
+		// So the stock decode is wrong twice: vec[1] as a hue gives a magenta/red screen, and
+		// vec[2] as a saturation is a coin flip -- a big positive clamps to 1.0 and night mode
+		// works by luck, a negative clamps to 0.0 and multiplies the tint away, leaving it
+		// visibly dead at every intensity.
+		//
+		// Take the two fields that survive the packing, pin the hue the wire loses, and guard
+		// both: the only validation on this path is the size()==3 check above, so a non-finite
+		// value would otherwise reach the HSV math unchecked.
+		//
+		// WATCH: if the client fixes its packing the wire becomes a correct
+		// [amount, hue, saturation] and this workaround starts mis-decoding -- reading the real
+		// hue as saturation. There is no reliable runtime sniff (a correct vec[2] and random
+		// heap bits are not distinguishable), so re-check this on Steam client updates.
+		{
+			constexpr float kNightModeAmberHue = 25.0f / 360.0f;  // 25deg amber, matches the x86 client
+			const float flAmount     = vec[0];
+			const float flSaturation = vec[1];
+			nightmode.amount     = std::isfinite( flAmount )     ? std::clamp( flAmount,     0.0f, 1.0f ) : 0.0f;
+			nightmode.saturation = std::isfinite( flSaturation ) ? std::clamp( flSaturation, 0.0f, 1.0f ) : 1.0f;
+			nightmode.hue        = kNightModeAmberHue;
+		}
+
 		if ( set_color_nightmode( nightmode ) )
 			hasRepaint = true;
 	}
